@@ -16,10 +16,10 @@ Use this package when you need to make POSIX system calls from Swift on macOS or
 - Waiting with a deadline polls with non-blocking attempts, sleeping 1 ms between them and doubling up to 100 ms. The deadline is measured on `Clock.Continuous`. A lock obtained after the deadline has passed is released again and reported as timed out.
 - `Clock.Continuous` keeps counting while the machine sleeps: CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW on Darwin, CLOCK_BOOTTIME on Linux. `Clock.Suspending` stops: CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW on Darwin, CLOCK_MONOTONIC on Linux. The CPU clocks report zero if the clock can't be read.
 - `Time.Zone.Rule.local` reads `TZ` again for every instant, so a change takes effect at once. If the local time can't be computed, it gives UTC. Its offsets are east of UTC, so `TZ=XXX-2` gives +2 hours.
-- `ISO_9945.Kernel.Thread.Condition.wait(mutex:timeout:)` returns false only when the timeout passes. Any other wakeup, spurious ones included, returns true, so check your condition again. The timeout runs on the monotonic clock on Linux and on the wall clock on Darwin.
+- `ISO_9945.Kernel.Thread.Condition.wait(mutex:timeout:)` returns false only when the timeout passes. Any other wakeup, spurious ones included, returns true, so check your condition again. The timeout is measured as elapsed time on both platforms, so a wall clock change does not stretch or cut it.
 - `ISO_9945.Kernel.Signal.Send.toGroup(_:pgid:)` refuses process group 1 with EINVAL, since that would signal every process. `ISO_9945.Kernel.Process.Wait.wait(_:options:)` returns nil only when you asked not to hang and no child has changed state.
 - An `ISO_9945.Kernel.Signal.Action.Handler.customInfo` handler is always installed with SA_SIGINFO and an `ISO_9945.Kernel.Signal.Action.Handler.custom` handler never is, whatever flags you give `ISO_9945.Kernel.Signal.Action.Configuration`. `ISO_9945.Kernel.Signal.Information.sender` is nil unless the signal came from kill or sigqueue or is a SIGCHLD about a child. `ISO_9945.Kernel.Signal.Information.fault` is set only for SIGSEGV, SIGBUS, SIGILL and SIGFPE raised by a fault.
-- Descriptor isolation for spawned processes differs by platform. On Linux, `ISO_9945.Kernel.Process.Spawn.Actions.isolate()` closes every descriptor from 3 up in the child, and spawning with `isolated: true` changes nothing. On Darwin, `isolated: true` passes the child only the descriptors the actions set up, resets every signal to its default and clears the signal mask, and `isolate()` adds standard input, output and error. With musl or glibc before 2.34, `isolate()` returns false.
+- Spawning with `isolated: true` resets every signal to its default and clears the signal mask on every platform. Descriptor isolation needs `ISO_9945.Kernel.Process.Spawn.Actions.isolate()` as well: together the child gets standard input, output and error and the descriptors the actions set up, and nothing else. On Darwin, `isolated: true` alone also withholds standard input, output and error; on Linux, `isolate()` alone closes every descriptor from 3 up. With musl or glibc before 2.34, `isolate()` returns false.
 - `ISO_9945.Kernel.Environment.set(_:to:overwrite:)` and `ISO_9945.Kernel.Environment.unset(_:)` refuse an empty name or a name containing `=` before calling the C library. `ISO_9945.Kernel.Environment.entries()` skips entries that have no `=`.
 - `ISO_9945.Kernel.User.Database` and `ISO_9945.Kernel.Group.Database` lookups return nil when there is no such entry and throw only when the lookup itself fails. `ISO_9945.Kernel.User.Login.name()` returns nil when the process has no controlling terminal.
 - Reading a symbolic link's target starts with a 256-byte buffer and doubles it up to 64 KiB. A longer target fails as too long for the buffer. The working directory is read the same way, from 4 KiB up to 1 MiB.
@@ -27,7 +27,7 @@ Use this package when you need to make POSIX system calls from Swift on macOS or
 - Only `ISO_9945.Kernel.Socket.Connect.connect(_:address:)` reports `interrupted`, and the connection attempt then goes on in the background. Every other `ISO_9945.Kernel.Socket.Error` is a platform error, an interrupted send or receive included.
 - `ISO_9945.Kernel.Termios.Attributes.withRaw()` turns off echo, canonical input, signal keys and output processing, sets 8-bit characters, and makes a read wait for at least one byte with no timeout.
 - Paths follow the POSIX grammar. Path text may not be empty or contain NUL or control characters. Components skip empty segments, so `a//b` has two. Appending joins with exactly one separator, and appending an absolute path gives that path. The parent of `/a` is `/`, and the root and a single relative component have none. A path relative to itself is `.`, and relative to a base that is not its prefix it is nil. A prefix must match whole components and be absolute or relative like the path.
-- `ISO_9945.Filename` is non-empty and has no `/`, NUL or control character (below 0x20, or 0x7F).
+- `ISO_9945.Filename` is non-empty and has no `/` or NUL. Built from text, it also has no control character (below 0x20, or 0x7F); built from code units with `init(platformNative:)`, as a directory entry hands it back, it keeps them. Only a literal makes a filename without throwing; interpolated text goes through the throwing `init(_:)`.
 - `ISO_9945.Utility.Name` is 2 to 9 characters, each a lowercase ASCII letter or a digit. An option name is one ASCII letter or digit.
 - `ISO_9945.Utility.Invocation.arguments` puts `--` before the operands only when the first operand starts with `-` and is longer than one character. A lone `-` is an operand.
 - `ISO_9945.Utility.Invocation.Coder` expands a grouped run such as `-ab` into one option per character. An option that takes an argument ends the run and takes the rest of the word, or the next word if nothing is left. Options end at `--`, which is dropped, or at the first word that is not an option. Writing an invocation back puts every option argument in its own word.
